1
00:00:03,141 --> 00:00:04,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, here we are.

2
00:00:05,622 --> 00:00:08,163
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's Sanguttara 514.

3
00:00:09,643 --> 00:00:16,046
[SPEAKER_00]: At one time, the Buddha was staying in the land of the Magandas at Andhaka Vinda.

4
00:00:16,866 --> 00:00:30,891
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, Venerable Ananda went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and the Buddha said to him, Ananda, those bhikkhus who are junior, recently gone forth, newly come to this teaching and discipline,

5
00:00:31,917 --> 00:00:34,799
[SPEAKER_00]: Should be encouraged, supported and established in five things.

6
00:00:36,861 --> 00:00:41,765
[SPEAKER_00]: They should be encouraged, supported and established in restraint by the monastic code.

7
00:00:43,186 --> 00:00:44,707
[SPEAKER_00]: Venerables, please be virtuous.

8
00:00:44,987 --> 00:00:46,528
[SPEAKER_00]: Live restrained in the monastic code.

9
00:00:47,289 --> 00:00:50,231
[SPEAKER_00]: Conducing yourselves well and seeking alms in suitable places.

10
00:00:50,551 --> 00:00:53,794
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing danger in the slightest fault, keep the rules you've undertaken.

11
00:00:55,792 --> 00:00:58,773
[SPEAKER_00]: They should be encouraged, supported and established in sense restraint.

12
00:00:59,453 --> 00:00:59,654
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

13
00:00:59,694 --> 00:00:59,814
[SPEAKER_00]: 2.

14
00:01:00,694 --> 00:01:08,357
[SPEAKER_00]: Venerables, please live with sense doors guarded, remembering to be judicious and on guard with the mind guarded by recollection.

15
00:01:10,858 --> 00:01:13,859
[SPEAKER_00]: They should be encouraged, supported and established in limiting the speech.

16
00:01:14,819 --> 00:01:18,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Venerables, speak little, put a limit on your speech.

17
00:01:21,022 --> 00:01:23,503
[SPEAKER_00]: They should be encouraged, supported, established in seclusion.

18
00:01:24,533 --> 00:01:26,815
[SPEAKER_00]: Venerables, please be forest dwellers.

19
00:01:27,255 --> 00:01:30,117
[SPEAKER_00]: Frequent remote lodgings in the wilderness and the forest.

20
00:01:32,258 --> 00:01:36,281
[SPEAKER_00]: They should be encouraged, supported, established in right perspective, in the right view.

21
00:01:36,561 --> 00:01:38,963
[SPEAKER_00]: Venerables, please have right view and right perspective.

22
00:01:40,644 --> 00:01:47,409
[SPEAKER_00]: So, those bhikkhus who are junior, recently gone forth, newly come to this teaching and discipline, should be encouraged, supported, established in these five things.

23
00:01:49,056 --> 00:01:51,682
[SPEAKER_00]: So, go back to the beginning, obviously.

24
00:01:51,922 --> 00:01:56,132
[SPEAKER_00]: The reason I picked this sutta, because it's... it is kind of one of the...

25
00:01:57,771 --> 00:02:03,054
[SPEAKER_00]: There are clear suttas that addresses people that are puthujjana, pretty much.

26
00:02:04,235 --> 00:02:17,284
[SPEAKER_00]: That says clearly, explicitly, those who are recently gone forth, those who are new to this, those who have not established themselves in the virtue as a sotāpanna and so on.

27
00:02:17,964 --> 00:02:23,568
[SPEAKER_00]: This is what they should be encouraged in, in order to develop the right view.

28
00:02:25,357 --> 00:02:28,605
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the first, the obvious, please be virtuous.

29
00:02:28,766 --> 00:02:31,011
[SPEAKER_00]: So, please live restrained by the precepts.

30
00:02:31,932 --> 00:02:33,573
[SPEAKER_00]: So, obviously, he talks about the monks here.

31
00:02:33,653 --> 00:02:36,974
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's referring to the monastic code.

32
00:02:37,534 --> 00:02:49,537
[SPEAKER_00]: But, generally, eight precepts, as in that which emulates the precepts of the monks, really, the basis of the holy life, how to say, is what constitutes virtue.

33
00:02:49,918 --> 00:02:55,139
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it is what constitutes that first thing that a person new to the teaching, i.e.

34
00:02:55,179 --> 00:02:57,640
[SPEAKER_00]: a pūtujana, should be encouraged in.

35
00:02:58,915 --> 00:03:10,539
[SPEAKER_00]: So, live restrained by the precepts, conduct yourself well, don't have unsuitable means of gaining livelihood and so on.

36
00:03:10,839 --> 00:03:21,083
[SPEAKER_00]: So, although it's, as I said, addressed to the monks, it can obviously be... the principles can be extrapolated onto anybody who wants to practice, whether they're a layperson or not, it doesn't matter.

37
00:03:21,963 --> 00:03:26,725
[SPEAKER_00]: So, seeing the danger in the slightest fault, in other words, take it on seriously, keep the rules you've undertaken,

38
00:03:28,287 --> 00:03:33,790
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's the beginning of that right training, towards the right view.

39
00:03:36,112 --> 00:03:39,394
[SPEAKER_00]: So, further, they should be encouraged, supported, established in sense restraint.

40
00:03:40,014 --> 00:03:42,656
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is now, like, on the level of...

41
00:03:42,676 --> 00:03:44,517
[SPEAKER_00]: So, okay, you're not breaking the virtue anymore.

42
00:03:44,557 --> 00:03:46,038
[SPEAKER_00]: You have been established in the precepts.

43
00:03:46,058 --> 00:03:47,940
[SPEAKER_00]: You are able to contain yourself within that.

44
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:49,961
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, on a more

45
00:03:51,642 --> 00:03:59,611
[SPEAKER_00]: At a subtler level, in a way, you need to be paying attention to what you choose to attend and what for.

46
00:04:00,452 --> 00:04:04,696
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, it becomes a bit more specific in the sense you're guarding the sense doors.

47
00:04:04,856 --> 00:04:07,719
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, even before you would have looked

48
00:04:08,440 --> 00:04:17,205
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, avoid unsuitable places, avoid unsuitable sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, because you know what will come through the sense doors.

49
00:04:27,216 --> 00:04:30,457
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, remember to be judicious and on guard.

50
00:04:31,217 --> 00:04:33,758
[SPEAKER_00]: So, to prevent things from arising.

51
00:04:33,878 --> 00:04:39,980
[SPEAKER_00]: Unnecessarily... to be unnecessarily bothered by hindrances, basically, and by mind overwhelmed by lust, aversion and so on.

52
00:04:40,480 --> 00:04:42,421
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that can be avoided if you guard your sense doors.

53
00:04:42,601 --> 00:04:45,902
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the mind is guarded by recollection.

54
00:04:46,502 --> 00:04:48,603
[SPEAKER_00]: It's guided by sati.

55
00:04:48,703 --> 00:04:50,183
[SPEAKER_00]: It's guided by that right context.

56
00:04:50,463 --> 00:04:52,124
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you know the context that you want to develop.

57
00:04:52,204 --> 00:04:53,384
[SPEAKER_00]: You know what you need to protect.

58
00:04:53,864 --> 00:04:56,105
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you will immediately know what's unsuitable for it.

59
00:04:57,918 --> 00:05:00,959
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, don't pretend that you don't know it.

60
00:05:01,759 --> 00:05:02,860
[SPEAKER_00]: Instead, use it to...

61
00:05:02,940 --> 00:05:04,180
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, no, I won't go there.

62
00:05:05,581 --> 00:05:14,744
[SPEAKER_00]: Although I might think I could handle it, the point is, I need to prevent the senses to even get in touch with these things, even if I can handle it.

63
00:05:15,524 --> 00:05:19,646
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there'll be plenty of opportunities that you can't prevent, and the senses will be bombarded.

64
00:05:20,466 --> 00:05:22,947
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you can practice your handling of it.

65
00:05:24,338 --> 00:05:27,786
[SPEAKER_00]: But there is no reason to give in to that when it's not necessary.

66
00:05:30,169 --> 00:05:33,451
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the third thing that should be encouraged support established in limiting the speech.

67
00:05:33,691 --> 00:05:35,533
[SPEAKER_00]: So, speak little, put limit on your speech.

68
00:05:35,833 --> 00:05:38,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, other suttas address this as well.

69
00:05:38,615 --> 00:05:45,799
[SPEAKER_00]: Speak truthfully, don't engage in pointless chit-chat, tail-bearing gossip, backstabbing, all that stuff.

70
00:05:45,819 --> 00:05:49,442
[SPEAKER_00]: Slander, you know, like, gets properly unwholesome.

71
00:05:50,082 --> 00:05:57,647
[SPEAKER_00]: So, because that's the pretty much the next layer of... that would be ripe for improvement, to put it that way.

72
00:05:59,168 --> 00:05:59,729
[SPEAKER_00]: Fourth thing.

73
00:05:59,929 --> 00:06:02,411
[SPEAKER_00]: They should be encouraged, supported and established in seclusion.

74
00:06:03,332 --> 00:06:04,873
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the actual physical seclusion.

75
00:06:05,353 --> 00:06:12,599
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if your environment does not necessarily always provide you opportunity for seclusion, well, whenever you can, you seek it.

76
00:06:12,639 --> 00:06:15,702
[SPEAKER_00]: So, frequent remote lodgings in the wilderness and the forest.

77
00:06:15,762 --> 00:06:16,983
[SPEAKER_00]: Even monks, see, frequent.

78
00:06:17,243 --> 00:06:24,449
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't necessarily mean go and live there permanently, but whenever you can, go there and spend time.

79
00:06:24,469 --> 00:06:27,952
[SPEAKER_00]: We'll see why that's important later, when we address the more specific questions.

80
00:06:29,428 --> 00:06:35,650
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and then the fifth thing that should be encouraged, supported and is venerable is please have the right view and the right perspective.

81
00:06:36,891 --> 00:06:41,833
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's kind of almost... Well, it can be, I guess, a bit confusing as in like, well, so what?

82
00:06:41,873 --> 00:06:44,694
[SPEAKER_00]: You just encourage somebody to have the right view and they'll have the right view.

83
00:06:44,774 --> 00:06:46,794
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, in a way, yes.

84
00:06:47,074 --> 00:06:54,157
[SPEAKER_00]: If they have completed and developed and purified all the previous steps that are clearly outlined in a certain sequence,

85
00:06:55,101 --> 00:07:00,582
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you don't need a lot of knowledge or theory to understand what the root of suffering is.

86
00:07:02,543 --> 00:07:19,606
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, all the theory that you know now, all the information that you have now, all the connections, parallels, insights that you have now, on account of studying the suttas, listening the talks... Well, the reason you're not sotāpanna is because of all these previous factors that you have not developed.

87
00:07:21,027 --> 00:07:21,407
[SPEAKER_00]: Because...

88
00:07:22,458 --> 00:07:25,240
[SPEAKER_00]: Very little is enough when the mind becomes pliable.

89
00:07:26,581 --> 00:07:34,648
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's the whole point of these things that a junior monk or a new to the training should be encouraged, is to make this mind pliable.

90
00:07:35,621 --> 00:07:43,806
[SPEAKER_00]: So that then, when you start reflecting on the root of suffering, you start thinking about a craving, it will be the craving and the root of suffering you're thinking of.

91
00:07:43,946 --> 00:07:54,754
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind will be seeing that, because the mind is not obscured, it's not as wild, and you could direct it, hold on, I need to understand this, and then the mind will stay on that.

92
00:07:55,454 --> 00:07:59,557
[SPEAKER_00]: As opposed to now, when you think you are directing it towards understanding, I don't know,

93
00:08:00,634 --> 00:08:04,157
[SPEAKER_00]: The root of suffering... the mind revolves around the idea of the root of suffering.

94
00:08:04,517 --> 00:08:06,098
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not the actual root of suffering.

95
00:08:06,239 --> 00:08:09,922
[SPEAKER_00]: Because for that, the mind needs to become pliable and less movable.

96
00:08:10,162 --> 00:08:11,123
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's... to put it that way.

97
00:08:12,324 --> 00:08:16,587
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why it's like... this... this sounds almost like an instruction, because it is an instruction.

98
00:08:16,627 --> 00:08:28,197
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, once you have tamed your mind sufficiently, when it's pliable enough, then, yeah, please, dwell, recollect, ponder what you learned about the right view.

99
00:08:28,926 --> 00:08:34,768
[SPEAKER_00]: about the Four Noble Truths, about what causes suffering.

100
00:08:34,908 --> 00:08:39,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Any aspect of the dhamma that comes up, the mind will be able to stay with it.

101
00:08:41,049 --> 00:08:46,151
[SPEAKER_00]: It won't drift off into abstraction and theory and the external standpoint to your own experience.

102
00:08:46,191 --> 00:08:47,971
[SPEAKER_00]: It will be there, applicable.

103
00:08:49,452 --> 00:08:54,013
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, that's why none of these steps are optional or negotiable, because

104
00:08:56,067 --> 00:09:01,730
[SPEAKER_00]: Sending the mind in the direction of understanding the dhamma, if that mind is not tamed and pliable, no such thing is possible.

105
00:09:01,770 --> 00:09:05,291
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, the same principle extends all the way to Arahantship, as other suttas would say.

106
00:09:05,971 --> 00:09:24,480
[SPEAKER_00]: Remember when he fully, like, fully purifies his mind from the hindrances, establishes on the level of jhāna, first jhāna, second jhāna, third jhāna, fourth jhāna, how the suttas would describe the whole thing, the maximum development, and then he says, he would just then direct his mind, if there were previous lives to remember, the mind would remember.

107
00:09:25,315 --> 00:09:28,258
[SPEAKER_00]: If the divine ear is to be developed, the mind would develop it.

108
00:09:28,498 --> 00:09:33,162
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, he would erect it to understand the root of all asavas, and abandon them all, and the mind would do it.

109
00:09:33,722 --> 00:09:40,448
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind would abandon it, because it has learned to listen to you, to trust you, and basically, above all, to not resist you.

110
00:09:42,610 --> 00:09:51,338
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the last part, it does involve active effort, in terms of trying to really understand what the root of dukkha is,

111
00:09:52,775 --> 00:10:03,025
[SPEAKER_00]: But it cannot possibly be done if the actual mind is not made pliable enough through the previous steps that have to remain there, maintained, protected, built upon.

112
00:10:06,088 --> 00:10:15,618
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why I picked this sutta, because the entire principle of this training, it doesn't matter if you're a monk, doesn't matter if you're a householder, it remains the same.

113
00:10:19,633 --> 00:10:28,156
[SPEAKER_00]: The pliability of the mind, through taming, restraint, mindfulness... Oh, and by the way, you might have noticed that there is absolutely no mention of meditation.

114
00:10:28,577 --> 00:10:30,417
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, not the concentration practices.

115
00:10:30,878 --> 00:10:32,558
[SPEAKER_00]: Not even mention of samādhi, for that matter.

116
00:10:33,459 --> 00:10:34,139
[SPEAKER_00]: Any in this sutta.

117
00:10:34,299 --> 00:10:35,139
[SPEAKER_00]: As any of the step.

118
00:10:36,100 --> 00:10:36,640
[SPEAKER_01]: Any of the steps.

119
00:10:40,161 --> 00:10:46,464
[SPEAKER_00]: Because how can you be composing and unifying your mind, whichever translation you want to use, if you're not even seeing your citta for what it is?

120
00:10:48,091 --> 00:10:56,179
[SPEAKER_00]: If you haven't even tamed it to understand it as that phenomenon, as that entity that lives in the heart of your being, as I often say.

121
00:10:58,722 --> 00:11:12,055
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the whole... well, I mean, we say that many times, that the whole practice today is just upside down, but the point is, even the right samādhi, there is no place for it here, because that can come only once there is actually right view, right perspective.

122
00:11:13,686 --> 00:11:16,069
[SPEAKER_00]: As many other suttas would say, and they say it exactly that.

123
00:11:16,189 --> 00:11:17,470
[SPEAKER_00]: The right view comes first.

124
00:11:17,991 --> 00:11:24,859
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in this case, the right view comes first as the beginning of the actual sotāpatti, the beginning of the actual knowledge of what the training is.

125
00:11:25,359 --> 00:11:28,883
[SPEAKER_00]: But you could see that the right view depends on these conditions that have to be developed.

126
00:11:28,964 --> 00:11:31,466
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you wonder, how do I begin practicing?

127
00:11:31,506 --> 00:11:32,427
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, wonder no more.

128
00:11:33,468 --> 00:11:48,373
[SPEAKER_00]: You begin by virtue, sense restraint, recollection, taking care of your speech, not letting slip or engage, allow unwholesome intentions to express themselves through speech, because obviously, okay, actions, let's say, you covered.

129
00:11:49,474 --> 00:11:51,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, apply the same thing to the mind.

130
00:11:51,394 --> 00:12:00,438
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the whole point of the seclusion, withdrawal, when your own thoughts get more amplified, that whole thing becomes more palpable, more visible, more...

131
00:12:01,629 --> 00:12:06,433
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, more acute, more real, less abstract, less fleeting.

132
00:12:07,914 --> 00:12:09,475
[SPEAKER_00]: It's harder to ignore it.

133
00:12:09,595 --> 00:12:10,896
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that comes with seclusion.

134
00:12:12,037 --> 00:12:17,281
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, yeah, you have clearly outlined steps that you could even do as a puṭṭhujana.

135
00:12:18,302 --> 00:12:28,690
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't need to be trying to get, you know, sotāpārthi through, like, yeah, well, I know sotāpārthi, practice starts with sotāpārthi, but I don't know how to get there, so I'm just doing everything and nothing.

136
00:12:29,476 --> 00:12:30,477
[SPEAKER_00]: It describes how you get there.

137
00:12:30,557 --> 00:12:42,126
[SPEAKER_00]: And, as I said, even when you think about sotāpatti now, even if you think in the right terms, the reason why that does not result in you becoming a sotāpanna is because the mind is not pliable enough.

138
00:12:42,406 --> 00:12:44,508
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do you... how do you make it pliable enough?

139
00:12:44,628 --> 00:12:48,551
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's pretty clear and pretty straightforward in a way.

140
00:12:49,632 --> 00:12:53,855
[SPEAKER_00]: But, eh... it does boil down to actual training and doing and repetition.

141
00:12:55,140 --> 00:12:56,001
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not given.

142
00:12:56,141 --> 00:12:57,242
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't fall from sky.

143
00:12:57,863 --> 00:13:00,985
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you just get the right view and then you do the right work and stuff like that.

144
00:13:06,771 --> 00:13:18,782
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the few questions that we also can ask on the basis of this sutta from the start.

145
00:13:20,185 --> 00:13:24,146
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, what does it mean to see the danger in the slightest fault?

146
00:13:25,446 --> 00:13:39,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, practically speaking, in your own life, whether you're a monk or a householder, does it mean, you know, becoming neurotic about everything and nothing?

147
00:13:41,091 --> 00:13:44,892
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know, everything is dangerous.

148
00:13:46,732 --> 00:13:48,673
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, there is some wisdom involved in this.

149
00:13:48,753 --> 00:13:55,938
[SPEAKER_00]: And yes, in the beginning, maybe you might be fearing everything, but fundamentally, it's like seeing the danger in the slightest fault.

150
00:13:56,098 --> 00:13:57,679
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, what's the danger?

151
00:13:57,939 --> 00:13:59,380
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the danger that you're seeing?

152
00:13:59,400 --> 00:14:00,141
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger of what?

153
00:14:00,681 --> 00:14:01,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you clear?

154
00:14:01,442 --> 00:14:02,142
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you clear with that?

155
00:14:03,563 --> 00:14:07,105
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're not, well, that's certainly a reflection you should be engaging in.

156
00:14:09,207 --> 00:14:11,528
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not dangerous just because the Buddha said so.

157
00:14:11,948 --> 00:14:14,410
[SPEAKER_00]: You need... you need to see the danger in the slightest fault.

158
00:14:16,379 --> 00:14:24,405
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether it's a big fault or the small fault, they should be the same thread of danger seen in either.

159
00:14:27,367 --> 00:14:29,748
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're not clear, well, you better become clear.

160
00:14:33,110 --> 00:14:40,676
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, as I said, so how can you then mirror this kind of restraint or precepts, moral commitments?

161
00:14:41,416 --> 00:14:43,438
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, we spoke about it many times.

162
00:14:44,301 --> 00:14:49,985
[SPEAKER_00]: You take on the precepts by body, you protect them, when you break them, you confess them.

163
00:14:51,707 --> 00:14:56,470
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't, you don't psychologize, reason and explain that I wasn't really affected, I had to do it.

164
00:14:56,530 --> 00:14:58,192
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter, even if you were not affected.

165
00:14:58,932 --> 00:15:01,254
[SPEAKER_00]: The point of confessing them is admitting.

166
00:15:01,554 --> 00:15:04,677
[SPEAKER_00]: See, breaking the precepts

167
00:15:05,499 --> 00:15:09,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Depending, you know, sometimes precept can be genuinely like a gray area.

168
00:15:10,142 --> 00:15:15,145
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not necessarily, I don't know, constituting an actual obstacle for the practice.

169
00:15:15,565 --> 00:15:19,508
[SPEAKER_00]: Say, you had to eat something in the afternoon because you went to some social function.

170
00:15:20,148 --> 00:15:27,093
[SPEAKER_00]: You didn't really want to, but it would have been just too weird and you didn't want to stand out and make your Buddhist principles known or whatever.

171
00:15:27,553 --> 00:15:29,915
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't want to be bothered, so you ate something.

172
00:15:30,975 --> 00:15:31,616
[SPEAKER_00]: You didn't mind.

173
00:15:32,436 --> 00:15:35,098
[SPEAKER_00]: See, that in and of itself would not necessarily be an obstacle.

174
00:15:36,040 --> 00:15:44,705
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, not admitting that there was against the precept, not confessing it, that's an obstacle.

175
00:15:44,725 --> 00:15:50,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Using your own reasoning to alleviate the weight of having to confess, having to restart this, that's an obstacle.

176
00:15:51,666 --> 00:16:07,959
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why whenever in the suttas monks or nuns broke the precepts, and then they saw the mistake of their ways, and then they would confess it, the Buddha would say, oh, very good that you confessed it, because confession of your mistake is a growth in your training as a noble disciple.

177
00:16:10,994 --> 00:16:12,976
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how you start to mirror this.

178
00:16:13,016 --> 00:16:25,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Not that you're never going to break the precepts, but, like, you have to take on that that weight of responsibility that comes with declaring that you're not going to break them, and then breaking them, and then confessing them.

179
00:16:28,650 --> 00:16:37,058
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, in that way, you are admitting that, yes, a certain behavior occurred which shouldn't have.

180
00:16:38,332 --> 00:16:42,675
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how the whole bodily domain gets perfected.

181
00:16:42,855 --> 00:16:47,938
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, after that, or concurrent to that, obviously, you'll be trying to develop everything in a way.

182
00:16:49,298 --> 00:16:52,100
[SPEAKER_00]: You keep an ionic speech and it goes with the same principle.

183
00:16:52,720 --> 00:16:57,143
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, like, the precept against the speech includes all the unwholesome forms of speech.

184
00:16:57,223 --> 00:16:58,764
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eh...

185
00:16:59,662 --> 00:17:06,466
[SPEAKER_00]: When upon reflection or memory or something, you recognize that, oh, yeah, you know, I've been talking carelessly or something, you don't justify it.

186
00:17:07,327 --> 00:17:15,692
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, but it wasn't against the precepts, because I wasn't, you know, I wasn't really angry, I wasn't really this... You recognize if it were, you admit it, and then you confess it.

187
00:17:18,574 --> 00:17:27,360
[SPEAKER_00]: But also keep in mind that, yes, the confession can be the growth towards the right practice, but keeping the precept does not mean purity in and of itself.

188
00:17:28,694 --> 00:17:32,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Because sometimes people think taking on the precepts purifies you, on its own.

189
00:17:33,616 --> 00:17:36,618
[SPEAKER_00]: Confessing, even when you break, regains that purity.

190
00:17:36,638 --> 00:17:37,638
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, no.

191
00:17:38,839 --> 00:17:42,741
[SPEAKER_00]: Taking on the precepts puts you in a position to begin purifying yourself.

192
00:17:44,201 --> 00:17:45,762
[SPEAKER_00]: In and of themselves, they do nothing.

193
00:17:46,882 --> 00:17:52,145
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they might give you a blameless existence here and now, as in you won't be getting into trouble.

194
00:17:53,942 --> 00:17:59,466
[SPEAKER_00]: But purification is not defined by precepts or how many you keep them or that you never break them.

195
00:18:00,647 --> 00:18:09,013
[SPEAKER_00]: It's defined by is your mind tamed on account of taking on the weight of the precepts, taking on the weight of the responsibility behind your actions and intentions and so on.

196
00:18:09,593 --> 00:18:11,255
[SPEAKER_00]: And like many monks fall into that trap.

197
00:18:11,515 --> 00:18:15,678
[SPEAKER_00]: They think by taking on the precepts and not breaking them and everything, they're pure.

198
00:18:16,218 --> 00:18:17,900
[SPEAKER_00]: In and of itself, that's what purity is.

199
00:18:18,660 --> 00:18:33,334
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why often many monks fall into this trap of thinking like, oh, if I can't keep the precepts according to my own abstract standard of this, like, purity that I think constitutes... that is defined by the precepts themselves, it's better for me to disrobe.

200
00:18:34,535 --> 00:18:36,717
[SPEAKER_00]: So, imagine the irony of it.

201
00:18:36,757 --> 00:18:39,159
[SPEAKER_00]: It's better for me to renounce the entire training

202
00:18:43,612 --> 00:18:51,596
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, to go against my perceived notion of being pure on account of simply keeping the precepts defined by others.

203
00:18:54,137 --> 00:19:00,780
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there are obvious precepts that are non-negotiable, but especially when it comes to monk's life, there are many that are just grey area and often depend on the intention.

204
00:19:02,541 --> 00:19:04,301
[SPEAKER_00]: And often, the intention is also ignored.

205
00:19:05,582 --> 00:19:11,805
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, it's like... Basically, people start getting off on that kind of high of

206
00:19:13,163 --> 00:19:25,697
[SPEAKER_00]: externally objectified idea of purity comes on account of this set of methods and behaviors, and if I stick to it, I'm pure, and I'm magically heading towards nibbāna, or whatever, and if I don't, I'm not pure.

207
00:19:26,798 --> 00:19:28,220
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, you're not pure, basically.

208
00:19:28,961 --> 00:19:30,242
[SPEAKER_00]: You're pure only when you're an arahant.

209
00:19:32,545 --> 00:19:34,647
[SPEAKER_00]: You're slightly less impure when you're a sotāpanna.

210
00:19:36,005 --> 00:19:41,668
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the whole point of that is to enable you to start actually purifying yourself in that right manner.

211
00:19:42,509 --> 00:19:42,669
[SPEAKER_00]: i.e.

212
00:19:42,909 --> 00:19:44,810
[SPEAKER_00]: on the level of the mind and your thoughts.

213
00:19:45,890 --> 00:19:48,992
[SPEAKER_00]: Not on the level of adhering to rules and duties.

214
00:19:49,292 --> 00:19:50,373
[SPEAKER_00]: Sīlabhāta paramāsa.

215
00:19:53,515 --> 00:20:03,480
[SPEAKER_00]: So, guarding of the sense doors in daily life, as I said, it involves foreseeing things before your sense doors are even open to them.

216
00:20:04,895 --> 00:20:14,539
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it does boil down to mindfulness, context, and recognizing that when you lose context, you basically lose guarding of the sense doors.

217
00:20:14,659 --> 00:20:15,079
[SPEAKER_00]: You forget.

218
00:20:16,860 --> 00:20:24,422
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not losing the context, you're guarding the sense doors, even if you are not needing to actively police your eyes, ears, nose, and so on.

219
00:20:26,303 --> 00:20:33,766
[SPEAKER_00]: And also means that, see, by guarding... by not losing the context, when you're guarding your sense doors, if you are then exposed

220
00:20:34,770 --> 00:20:39,492
[SPEAKER_00]: Against your will, to sights, sounds, you still guard your doors.

221
00:20:40,332 --> 00:20:42,513
[SPEAKER_00]: Those things are still not coming in because you haven't lost the context.

222
00:20:43,373 --> 00:20:51,996
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't guard the doors by, I don't know, in a bus you hear a music and go and pull the... rip the cords of the of the radio so that speakers... destroy the speakers or something to protect your senses.

223
00:20:52,096 --> 00:20:52,216
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

224
00:20:52,276 --> 00:20:53,256
[SPEAKER_00]: Just don't lose the context.

225
00:20:54,777 --> 00:20:55,857
[SPEAKER_00]: And you are protecting your senses.

226
00:21:01,750 --> 00:21:11,813
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the speech part, then, as I said, you, like, simply refraining from pointless speech, it's gonna have layers and layers you're gonna have to peel off.

227
00:21:11,833 --> 00:21:19,115
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, for somebody who hasn't been restraining the speech, just refraining from saying stuff initially, it's gonna feel like a massive, massive step forward.

228
00:21:19,175 --> 00:21:23,176
[SPEAKER_00]: But once you get used to that, you're gonna recognize, okay, there's a further development to be done here.

229
00:21:24,256 --> 00:21:27,437
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's also how you start to notice what kind of effect

230
00:21:28,740 --> 00:21:35,743
[SPEAKER_00]: Speaking less, speaking less carelessly, less pointlessly, what kind of effect that has on the citta?

231
00:21:37,664 --> 00:21:51,570
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, your citta will be not as obscured and you can become more and more familiar with the signs and characteristics that define it.

232
00:21:51,590 --> 00:21:51,810
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

233
00:21:53,462 --> 00:21:54,923
[SPEAKER_00]: And same with guarding of the sense doors.

234
00:21:55,183 --> 00:21:56,963
[SPEAKER_00]: When it comes to speech, you can also foresee.

235
00:21:57,344 --> 00:22:01,265
[SPEAKER_00]: You could see a... you could start talking, and it can start on the basis of a wholesome intention.

236
00:22:02,545 --> 00:22:09,268
[SPEAKER_00]: But then you notice you're getting caught up in what's being spoken, back and forth, and whatever else, and the intention starts to change.

237
00:22:10,168 --> 00:22:11,969
[SPEAKER_00]: And you foresee that, and you stop it.

238
00:22:13,410 --> 00:22:19,432
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, yes, you have to endure that built-up pressure on account of... let me just say one more thing, and stuff like that.

239
00:22:19,652 --> 00:22:20,312
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's the training.

240
00:22:20,772 --> 00:22:22,013
[SPEAKER_00]: Putting the limit on speech.

241
00:22:22,865 --> 00:22:23,525
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you do it.

242
00:22:27,548 --> 00:22:30,850
[SPEAKER_00]: So, dwell in seclusion when you can.

243
00:22:31,250 --> 00:22:37,353
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the purpose of seclusion, as I said, as I already mentioned when we read the Sutta, is to amplify.

244
00:22:38,014 --> 00:22:48,880
[SPEAKER_00]: Amplify whatever is going on in your citta, whatever pressures are there, become more revealed, whatever inclinations are coming up, whatever moods, whatever shifts, all of that,

245
00:22:50,738 --> 00:23:00,646
[SPEAKER_00]: Inseclusion becomes highlighted, because in seclusion you don't have... well, the distractions and things to engage with are not at the arm's length.

246
00:23:02,308 --> 00:23:12,957
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if you say slip, in terms of mentally you start to entertain unwholesome thoughts and this or that, well, you won't be able to slip too far back.

247
00:23:14,049 --> 00:23:20,272
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're in seclusion, or at least if you're frequenting and frequently spending time in that seclusion.

248
00:23:20,912 --> 00:23:23,554
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why physical solitude is necessary to some extent.

249
00:23:24,587 --> 00:23:29,471
[SPEAKER_00]: And even, yeah, even in other suttas where the Buddha said, eh... what did he say?

250
00:23:30,131 --> 00:23:33,954
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, to become a sotāpanna without grasping signs of your mind, no such thing is possible.

251
00:23:34,374 --> 00:23:41,179
[SPEAKER_00]: To grasp the signs of your mind without having developed at least some degree of delighting and seclusion, no such thing is possible.

252
00:23:41,419 --> 00:23:47,583
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you won't be seeing your citta if you're constantly dwelling in an environment that's crowded and dusty, as the suttas would say.

253
00:23:48,904 --> 00:23:54,048
[SPEAKER_00]: So, obviously, for monks seclusion is a bit easier, but not necessarily, depending on which monastery you live in.

254
00:23:55,300 --> 00:23:59,541
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes you actually have more say in your seclusion when you're a layman.

255
00:23:59,622 --> 00:24:10,085
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can change the monasteries, you can change the places, but as a layman, yeah, whenever you have the opportunity for seclusion, do not neglect it, even if it doesn't feel like a big deal.

256
00:24:12,634 --> 00:24:16,735
[SPEAKER_00]: Dwelling in it, getting used to it, means basically getting used to the signs of your own mind.

257
00:24:17,395 --> 00:24:25,077
[SPEAKER_00]: Especially if it's not just you you jump into seclusion or have views that seclusion will magically on its own just make you understand the dhamma.

258
00:24:25,597 --> 00:24:30,799
[SPEAKER_00]: But no, you are virtuous, you are practicing restraint, you are guarding the sense doors, you are limiting your speech.

259
00:24:31,879 --> 00:24:36,440
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, even if you don't see a direct link between seclusion and stuff, do it.

260
00:24:37,581 --> 00:24:38,321
[SPEAKER_01]: Whenever you can.

261
00:24:39,641 --> 00:24:42,262
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the benefits will become apparent.

262
00:24:43,812 --> 00:24:51,360
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the right view then, as I already said, you can be encouraging.

263
00:24:51,400 --> 00:24:52,462
[SPEAKER_00]: Please develop right view.

264
00:24:52,502 --> 00:24:59,850
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't neglect those reflections, because the mind is already pliable enough, or at least working in that direction to become so.

265
00:25:01,092 --> 00:25:14,219
[SPEAKER_00]: So, with the pliable... with the mind pliable enough, as I said, you do not need refine sort of subtle concepts of the connections, the aggregates, the this, the that.

266
00:25:15,079 --> 00:25:18,201
[SPEAKER_00]: You could just literally start asking yourself questions, even out loud.

267
00:25:19,021 --> 00:25:19,882
[SPEAKER_00]: What is suffering?

268
00:25:22,063 --> 00:25:22,723
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, what is it?

269
00:25:23,363 --> 00:25:25,504
[SPEAKER_00]: When I say suffering, what am I referring to?

270
00:25:25,905 --> 00:25:26,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Here and now.

271
00:25:27,927 --> 00:25:30,389
[SPEAKER_00]: When I remember suffering, what is it that I remember?

272
00:25:30,809 --> 00:25:31,850
[SPEAKER_00]: What am I designating?

273
00:25:31,910 --> 00:25:37,134
[SPEAKER_00]: What phenomenon is there that stands for this suffering that I'm talking about or trying to free myself?

274
00:25:40,036 --> 00:25:46,680
[SPEAKER_00]: And, see, those questions now, paired with the pliable mind, they send the mind in that direction.

275
00:25:49,182 --> 00:25:50,163
[SPEAKER_00]: That direction was always there.

276
00:25:50,223 --> 00:25:51,143
[SPEAKER_00]: Suffering was always there.

277
00:25:51,243 --> 00:25:53,205
[SPEAKER_00]: But you never sat with it, so to speak.

278
00:25:54,206 --> 00:25:56,027
[SPEAKER_00]: You never made your mind sit with it.

279
00:26:00,464 --> 00:26:03,605
[SPEAKER_00]: Ask yourself, well, the Buddha said, tanhā is the root of suffering.

280
00:26:03,685 --> 00:26:04,285
[SPEAKER_00]: What is tanhā?

281
00:26:04,905 --> 00:26:05,645
[SPEAKER_00]: What is thirst?

282
00:26:05,705 --> 00:26:06,386
[SPEAKER_00]: What is craving?

283
00:26:06,426 --> 00:26:08,446
[SPEAKER_00]: What is whatever I translated or non-translated?

284
00:26:08,986 --> 00:26:10,026
[SPEAKER_00]: What am I referring to?

285
00:26:12,607 --> 00:26:16,348
[SPEAKER_00]: In this experience, here and now, as a whole, what phenomena is there?

286
00:26:17,448 --> 00:26:20,469
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it's vague, there has to be something I'm talking about.

287
00:26:20,589 --> 00:26:20,989
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it?

288
00:26:22,830 --> 00:26:23,910
[SPEAKER_00]: Let me understand that.

289
00:26:26,102 --> 00:26:39,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you try that before, it's gonna fall into psychologizations, philosophizing, theory, connections in space and time, or structures, the aggregates, this is because of that.

290
00:26:40,571 --> 00:26:41,491
[SPEAKER_00]: But you haven't sat with it.

291
00:26:42,172 --> 00:26:44,653
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you sit with it, you don't need to talk much, so to speak.

292
00:26:45,794 --> 00:26:51,137
[SPEAKER_00]: You already did the work of taming the mind, making it pliable enough.

293
00:26:52,658 --> 00:26:54,299
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, even rightly directed questions,

294
00:26:55,920 --> 00:26:57,721
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what the sutta encourages.

295
00:26:59,162 --> 00:27:00,362
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, please, have the right view.

296
00:27:00,382 --> 00:27:03,484
[SPEAKER_00]: Develop it, so to have it.

297
00:27:14,786 --> 00:27:29,794
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I was reminded of something when you were talking about the keeping the precepts and then having this idea that precepts by themselves or keeping the precepts by themselves will somehow result in your becoming pure.

298
00:27:30,474 --> 00:27:44,082
[SPEAKER_01]: And I was reminded there's this story I came across in one of the books by Viktor Frankl where he talks about it's happening in the First World War and there's a

299
00:27:44,842 --> 00:27:58,313
[SPEAKER_01]: German general standing in the trenches, and beside this German general, there is a Jewish doctor covering and trembling with fear, because, you know, the opposite side is shooting constantly.

300
00:27:58,953 --> 00:28:00,414
[SPEAKER_01]: And the German general says,

301
00:28:01,592 --> 00:28:18,140
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, look at you, you know, the Jew, being spineless and afraid, you know, cowering and trembling and standing there, all supposedly courageous and so on and so forth, and

302
00:28:19,241 --> 00:28:30,070
[SPEAKER_01]: The doctor says, well, you know, you said that I'm afraid and that I'm here cowering and don't have the courage that you have.

303
00:28:30,530 --> 00:28:38,256
[SPEAKER_01]: But I assure you, if you felt so much fear as I do, I can guarantee you that you'll be long gone from here.

304
00:28:38,957 --> 00:28:41,639
[SPEAKER_01]: And the main idea behind this is that

305
00:28:42,760 --> 00:28:56,492
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not so much about, you know, not, for example, in this particular context, sensing fear, but being able to actually position yourself in such a way that you don't respond to it.

306
00:28:57,012 --> 00:28:57,813
[SPEAKER_01]: And the same thing

307
00:28:58,513 --> 00:28:59,233
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, precisely.

308
00:28:59,373 --> 00:29:04,094
[SPEAKER_01]: And the same thing with regards to what you were saying with the precepts.

309
00:29:04,735 --> 00:29:15,217
[SPEAKER_01]: There are people who, for some reason, one reason or another, who would understand these complex karmic connections, they don't have issues with following the precepts.

310
00:29:15,437 --> 00:29:17,478
[SPEAKER_01]: It just comes somehow easier to them.

311
00:29:17,818 --> 00:29:19,238
[SPEAKER_01]: And there are people who struggle a lot.

312
00:29:19,678 --> 00:29:28,060
[SPEAKER_01]: And the idea is, again, from what you were saying, it is about the attitude you take and the understanding that you gain from what is happening in the overall process.

313
00:29:28,540 --> 00:29:34,105
[SPEAKER_01]: And this particular story seems to be a very good exemplification of precisely that.

314
00:29:34,345 --> 00:29:41,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not about, you know, maybe by nature, you're not afraid if somebody's shooting around you.

315
00:29:42,392 --> 00:29:42,612
[SPEAKER_01]: But...

316
00:29:43,513 --> 00:29:48,937
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were afraid, if you were trembling like that person, would you still persist or would you run away?

317
00:29:49,197 --> 00:29:50,558
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

318
00:29:50,578 --> 00:30:04,649
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in this particular sense, I think that what you were trying to say, it is the attitude that allows you to develop the overall poise, the overall stance, existential, and the understanding that you gain from that.

319
00:30:04,669 --> 00:30:08,592
[SPEAKER_01]: That this is the purity that comes from keeping the purity.

320
00:30:08,752 --> 00:30:09,072
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

321
00:30:09,112 --> 00:30:12,895
[SPEAKER_00]: So, a person, basically, that feels

322
00:30:13,478 --> 00:30:34,477
[SPEAKER_00]: affected by keeping the precepts is closer to the development of purity than the person who doesn't, because they took them on account of tremendous inspiration, faith, the authority of Ajahn, the tradition, the culture, the philosophical reasoning.

323
00:30:35,058 --> 00:30:37,660
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, if precepts are taken rightly,

324
00:30:38,946 --> 00:30:42,148
[SPEAKER_00]: At any given time, you recognize that you cannot keep them, if you don't want to.

325
00:30:42,768 --> 00:30:51,113
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no external authority or value that dictates keeping the precepts in their own terms.

326
00:30:51,133 --> 00:30:53,074
[SPEAKER_00]: It's you choose to do it, because you chose to do it.

327
00:30:54,255 --> 00:30:57,556
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you take it that way, then they feel a lot different.

328
00:30:59,237 --> 00:31:01,699
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you realize, I could just leave.

329
00:31:01,859 --> 00:31:02,719
[SPEAKER_00]: I could just walk out.

330
00:31:04,400 --> 00:31:07,242
[SPEAKER_00]: I could just turn away from this next second.

331
00:31:08,308 --> 00:31:09,269
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's frightening.

332
00:31:09,649 --> 00:31:10,410
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I could run.

333
00:31:12,731 --> 00:31:13,832
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like, but I don't.

334
00:31:14,152 --> 00:31:16,093
[SPEAKER_00]: I choose not to, every single time.

335
00:31:16,874 --> 00:31:21,637
[SPEAKER_00]: But see, it's a lot easier to take on the precepts and place the weight of keeping them on the environment.

336
00:31:21,978 --> 00:31:25,000
[SPEAKER_00]: I keep them because I'll get in trouble if I don't, here in this monastery.

337
00:31:27,001 --> 00:31:32,345
[SPEAKER_00]: I keep them because the vast amount of people say I should do so.

338
00:31:34,059 --> 00:31:37,161
[SPEAKER_00]: I keep them because I believe it will help my meditation technique or whatever.

339
00:31:37,781 --> 00:31:44,765
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, I don't keep them because I choose to keep them every single day whenever I'm reminded of breaking them.

340
00:31:45,206 --> 00:31:46,626
[SPEAKER_00]: I choose to not break them.

341
00:31:47,767 --> 00:31:49,348
[SPEAKER_00]: Then that edge is felt a lot closer.

342
00:31:49,788 --> 00:31:50,929
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's actually the training.

343
00:31:50,989 --> 00:31:55,952
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, you don't need to go all the time with that edge, but you need to not run away.

344
00:31:56,902 --> 00:31:58,824
[SPEAKER_00]: When the weight of the precepts is felt on you.

345
00:31:58,964 --> 00:31:59,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's what people would do.

346
00:32:00,205 --> 00:32:00,786
[SPEAKER_00]: People feel it.

347
00:32:00,866 --> 00:32:09,354
[SPEAKER_00]: I bet that German soldier felt the fear and then justified it with his superiority or whatever ideas, convictions placed him in that position.

348
00:32:09,995 --> 00:32:11,516
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what would happen to the monks.

349
00:32:11,536 --> 00:32:14,519
[SPEAKER_00]: They would take on the precepts and you feel it.

350
00:32:15,180 --> 00:32:17,002
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, you learn the ways

351
00:32:18,033 --> 00:32:27,600
[SPEAKER_00]: In the environment, in the tradition, in this... And then, you actively make the effort to not think about you choosing to keep them every time, but to go along with them.

352
00:32:29,381 --> 00:32:30,522
[SPEAKER_00]: And it feels a lot easier.

353
00:32:31,342 --> 00:32:33,423
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a lot less weight on you.

354
00:32:34,524 --> 00:32:41,489
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, if you, every single time, you recognize that the sole reason for keeping them is because you keep choosing to do so.

355
00:32:44,011 --> 00:32:47,073
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why I said, there's like a useful reflection

356
00:32:48,495 --> 00:32:54,040
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, actually, a useful reflection that both lay people and monks can do.

357
00:32:54,800 --> 00:33:09,372
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for monks, like, you should ask yourself, if, say, being a monk is made illegal, and I have to abandon this form, I have to leave the monastery, I have to take the robes off, would my values remain unchanged?

358
00:33:09,852 --> 00:33:10,953
[SPEAKER_00]: Or would I feel relieved?

359
00:33:12,134 --> 00:33:14,536
[SPEAKER_00]: Would I feel absolved from keeping the precepts now?

360
00:33:15,991 --> 00:33:17,792
[SPEAKER_00]: And that shows you exactly where your values are.

361
00:33:18,393 --> 00:33:19,614
[SPEAKER_00]: Because nothing should change.

362
00:33:20,474 --> 00:33:27,840
[SPEAKER_00]: If you want to be free from suffering and practice the dhamma, there should be no internal change whatsoever, whether you wear robes or not.

363
00:33:28,160 --> 00:33:29,421
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's even that sutra which says that.

364
00:33:29,822 --> 00:33:35,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Monks, if wearing the robes would make you free from avarice and all this, I would say, yes, go and wear robes and stuff like that.

365
00:33:36,407 --> 00:33:42,071
[SPEAKER_00]: For a lay person who is not committed to that level, but wishes to be committed or thinks he is committed,

366
00:33:43,818 --> 00:33:51,140
[SPEAKER_00]: To the level of training, obviously, you know, he's not wearing robes, he doesn't follow that same form, but he should ask himself the opposite question, from the opposite direction.

367
00:33:51,200 --> 00:33:57,762
[SPEAKER_00]: Say, I want to be a monk, but I can't, because I have a wife, I have children, I have to support them.

368
00:33:58,243 --> 00:33:59,443
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a valid reason.

369
00:33:59,703 --> 00:34:02,084
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, they will go to ruin if I leave.

370
00:34:03,204 --> 00:34:12,687
[SPEAKER_00]: However, ask yourself, that person should ask themselves, if my wife were to come tomorrow and tell me, I actually found another man,

371
00:34:13,780 --> 00:34:17,101
[SPEAKER_00]: And he's gonna look after your children like they're his own.

372
00:34:18,842 --> 00:34:20,042
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm gonna be obviously fine with him.

373
00:34:20,182 --> 00:34:20,823
[SPEAKER_00]: I prefer him.

374
00:34:21,243 --> 00:34:22,223
[SPEAKER_00]: You are free to go.

375
00:34:23,484 --> 00:34:26,505
[SPEAKER_00]: Would you be relieved by that news?

376
00:34:26,805 --> 00:34:29,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Or would you feel, oh, wait, hold on, let's talk about this.

377
00:34:30,726 --> 00:34:32,947
[SPEAKER_00]: And that shows you where your actual values are.

378
00:34:33,267 --> 00:34:37,249
[SPEAKER_00]: Regardless of what you tell yourself, no, no, no, I become a monk the moment I'm free.

379
00:34:38,850 --> 00:34:40,650
[SPEAKER_00]: Imagine there is a moment you're free.

380
00:34:40,770 --> 00:34:41,250
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you ready?

381
00:34:41,731 --> 00:34:42,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you should be.

382
00:34:43,279 --> 00:34:44,059
[SPEAKER_00]: And same for monks.

383
00:34:44,099 --> 00:34:46,000
[SPEAKER_00]: Imagine a moment you can't continue being a monk.

384
00:34:46,921 --> 00:34:47,421
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you ready?

385
00:34:49,402 --> 00:35:00,066
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you know precepts are kept on the right level, in line with the dhamma, and actually are resulting in training, taming, pliability.

386
00:35:00,086 --> 00:35:04,388
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you have a good reason to not become a monk, fine.

387
00:35:05,548 --> 00:35:09,950
[SPEAKER_00]: But that does not excuse you from mentally developing the same framework.

388
00:35:11,255 --> 00:35:15,057
[SPEAKER_00]: If you have a valid reason to abandon robes, I don't know, war or something, fine.

389
00:35:15,777 --> 00:35:21,439
[SPEAKER_00]: But that does not excuse you from carrying on the same virtue, the same restraint, the same practice.

390
00:35:22,100 --> 00:35:25,981
[SPEAKER_00]: But more often than not, the entire thing is associated with what you wear.

391
00:35:27,582 --> 00:35:28,983
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the authority comes from.

392
00:35:29,963 --> 00:35:31,003
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's taken away.

393
00:35:31,104 --> 00:35:36,346
[SPEAKER_00]: People have no internal means to continue in the same direction, because they never developed them.

394
00:35:37,172 --> 00:35:45,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Precepts were always kept for them by the tradition, by the culture, by the inspiration, by everything else but myself.

395
00:35:52,746 --> 00:35:54,847
[SPEAKER_01]: In a certain sense, they come with a package.

396
00:35:55,007 --> 00:36:14,473
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about using them as means for you to be able to actually engage with, for example, as means that would disclose or reveal the propulsion of greed or repulsion of hatred or the scatteredness of delusion.

397
00:36:16,021 --> 00:36:28,166
[SPEAKER_01]: By preventing you this or that, in a very concrete, specific situation, so the the initial steps into the wisdom, but they are literally just forms that you, like... Yeah, they become...

398
00:36:28,186 --> 00:36:37,290
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if somebody initially feels that that weight of that responsibility, as I said, if they're not cultivating it, it's just going to become a form.

399
00:36:37,430 --> 00:36:37,610
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

400
00:36:38,961 --> 00:36:43,663
[SPEAKER_01]: So, do they contextualize it in a way of, ah, this is just, you know, part of this embeddedness?

401
00:36:43,823 --> 00:36:44,144
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

402
00:36:44,244 --> 00:36:48,526
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it's not incidental either, that purity becomes synonymous with it.

403
00:36:50,126 --> 00:36:59,411
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, if I sustain this form, divorced from that pressure, that's what defines purity.

404
00:36:59,451 --> 00:37:00,131
[SPEAKER_00]: Therefore, I'm pure.

405
00:37:00,451 --> 00:37:07,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether I make the effort towards dhamma or not, it would be good if I do, but if I don't, either way, I'm purified by keeping them.

406
00:37:09,053 --> 00:37:14,795
[SPEAKER_00]: To the point of, you even have, like, like, almost like a monk's developed superstitious, superstitiousness on account of it.

407
00:37:15,995 --> 00:37:25,678
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereby, I make zero effort to tame the mind, or understand the dhamma, but I take these precepts religiously, because that's the protection, the paritta, the protection charms and stuff, comes from being virtuous.

408
00:37:25,718 --> 00:37:34,020
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, yes, sure, comes you, comes from not doing harm, but you know when you're safe from saṃsara, or, or doing bad stuff ever again, because of it.

409
00:37:34,441 --> 00:37:35,781
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're not uprooting anything.

410
00:37:38,607 --> 00:37:57,654
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to basically disassociate your idea of purity being on the level of just this mere form, and recognize that, no, that just enables me to actually do something about it, and develop that purity, which can only come if things are uprooted from where they start, which is your citta, your mind.

411
00:37:59,235 --> 00:38:03,217
[SPEAKER_00]: Needs to be tamed.

412
00:38:03,237 --> 00:38:04,657
[SPEAKER_01]: You said the last thing, basically.

413
00:38:07,885 --> 00:38:22,317
[SPEAKER_01]: So, someone who would initially maybe be confessing a lot, might actually be long-term, mid to long-term, more pure than someone who might, you know... Getting closer to the actual purity, yes.

414
00:38:22,658 --> 00:38:23,899
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, that's what I mean, yeah.

415
00:38:24,439 --> 00:38:24,619
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

416
00:38:24,639 --> 00:38:27,201
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't mean you should go and actively try and break the precepts or something.

417
00:38:27,221 --> 00:38:27,702
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, of course.

418
00:38:27,982 --> 00:38:34,808
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, you know, you're gonna come close, you're gonna make mistakes, but when you do, you don't cover that up either.

419
00:38:36,488 --> 00:38:39,351
[SPEAKER_00]: And certainly don't start thinking you're pure because you confessed it.

420
00:38:40,352 --> 00:38:45,598
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, okay, I uttered the words, therefore purity is regained.

421
00:38:46,559 --> 00:38:51,705
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I uttered the words, therefore ability to do something about it is regained.

